  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    A seven-tiered aurora-light repository carved into pale blue ice on the upper reaches of Aurora Stack. The Dawn Terrace was discovered during the Great Thaw by the outlaw Iing zhixai'Ward, whose surveying marks remain visible in the deepest cuts — his initials scratched backward, a signature legible only to himself. The structure's crystal galleries channel aurora-light with a precision that suggests either extraordinary engineering or properties inherent to the ice itself. The Terrace has passed through the control of three factions, survived two major military engagements, and now operates as a commercial tourist destination under the Icebound Exchange, a governing trade authority of Aurora Stack. It is, by local consensus, the closest thing to a vacation on The Berg, marketed to an audience with nowhere else to go.
    
    The ambient temperature holds at 48 degrees — warm enough that the ice weeps in summer, cold enough that frostbite follows prolonged stillness.
    
    ## Occupational History
    
    The sequence of control is legible in the ice itself, each occupant's tenure written over the last.
    
    **The Wake-Singers** — an orca shaman cult devoted to waking the dead god through accumulated blood sacrifice — held the Terrace first, or at least earliest among documented occupants. Their fire-core rituals in crystal basins left a sub-audible vibration in the upper galleries that persists to this day; the aurora-responsive acoustics produce a clean, near-musical tone above, and a lower frequency beneath it that affects the teeth and sternum. Blood-marks from Wake-Singer rites survive on the Tier 3 passage walls, polished over by later occupants with volcanic stone that does not match the surrounding ice.
    
    **The E'frostlings**, Aurora Stack's dominant merchant faction, seized the Terrace and held it long enough to leave utilitarian marks: tool-racks bolted into walls, a sleeping chamber carved with deliberate ugliness, a shrine to something that was not the aurora. The E'frostlings attempted to seal corruption damage with volcanic stone — the same wrong-colored material used to obscure the Wake-Singer blood-marks, a coincidence of method that the records do not remark upon but the ice preserves plainly.
    
    E'frostling control ended with the **Frost-heir succession crisis**, when the death of Aurora Stack's eldest mystic mid-ritual shattered the crystalline hierarchy. The Tertiary Spire collapsed during the ensuing violence, killing three claimants: Qingyaieifu, Shanfongqongja Teir'Gleam, and Ouchangzhaijun. The Flipper Accord — the trade framework binding Aurora Stack and Nightfall Shelf — nearly broke when one faction threatened to sever fire-core shipments.
    
    ## Military Engagements
    
    | Event | Era | Combatants | Outcome |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | **Vum∴tenebra** | Faction Wars | ☽'shadow Covens' Order vs. E'frostling positions | Crystal stockpiles destroyed; Flipper Accord rendered inoperable; seventeen confirmed dead. Char, brine, and fire-core fragments fused into gallery walls. |
    | **Gore-ruin** (orca offensive) | Orca Incursion | Orca shore-breakers vs. Midnight Claws (dissolved Nightshelf unit) | Upper spires shattered; three defensive tiers collapsed. Midnight Claws cast Grrul-kzul-strike from the trade-platform. Eastern ridge blown outward. Corruption scar spread from casting point; aurora rhythm above the Terrace permanently diminished. |
    
    The Vum∴tenebra's memorial stones at the frozen edge bear names that go unvisited. Watchers were reported in surrounding tunnels in the weeks following — their nature unspecified in any record willing to speculate. The Midnight Claws' action at Gore-ruin was unauthorized; the unit had already been formally dissolved. That a Nightshelf intelligence operation — unacknowledged then, still unacknowledged — performed the decisive defense of an Aurora site is a fact the official histories have managed not to notice.
    
    ## Corruption and Anomalous Properties
    
    Corruption from The Still Pools, a nearby breach-point in the ice, crept into the Terrace gradually. Its progression is mapped in the frost-patterning along Tiers 3 and 4: gray discoloration, brittle formations, fissures that resisted every sealing attempt. The Foundation ice from adjacent strata confirms the Still Pools corruption predates any faction's occupation of this site.
    
    The lower vaults are closed to visitors indefinitely. The third crystal pillar on the left in the Tier 5 gallery produces a frequency that has affected visitors for cycles after exposure — the Icebound Exchange's brochures term this "ambient resonance." Visitors report a patient, non-penguin presence watching from the corrupted ice. Staff carry pre-printed response cards for these complaints. Something vast moved through during the Frozen Peace. The magic manifested twice. No record specifies what was seen, and the consistency of the refusal to describe it is itself a kind of testimony.
    
    ## Artifacts and Present Use
    
    Two significant artifacts remain on-site:
    
    - **The Scared Shroud**, located by Glow-Trader Vemlaus-vel in its vault and untouched since the Frost-heir line was severed. Locals call the Terrace "the Broken Crown" — a name that refers to the Shroud as much as to the site.
    - **The Thaw-Paths Medallion**, enshrined under Stillness-Bound law. It glows without aurora-light on the anniversary of the breach at The Fissured Smelt~, a catastrophic Wake-Singer incursion. The pattern it displays matches nothing on The Berg, or everything, depending on the observer.
    
    The Icebound Exchange now operates the Terrace commercially: heritage walks, krill stalls staffed by Nightshelf vendors using imported fire-cores, warming stations across the tiers. Visitor reviews note the omission of zhixai'Ward's name from interpretive materials. The Exchange's language around closed sections and unexplained phenomena has drawn scrutiny from those who suspect the guided experience is designed to show precisely as much as is profitable and no more.
    
    In the lower Nightshelf, children's counting-rhymes cast the Terrace as a bright, forbidden place from which the curious do not return. The rhymes count to eighteen. The arithmetic does not hold. Adults, I am told, do not like to hear this noted.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
